nd the child's own exit, and round 2 armed the settle from either -- so a command that exited 0 in ~50ms while a process that had escaped its group (os.setsid) kept the INHERITED stdout open was REJECTED at killGraceMs, with a message naming a 600000ms timeout that never happened. A label nothing derives, inside a refusal. REPRODUCED BEFORE FIXING, and my first probe was INVALID AND IS DISCLOSED: it used `setsid bash -c`, and macOS ships no setsid(1), so it silently created no out-of-group holder and reported RESOLVED at 5217ms -- a false pass that looked like non-reproduction. Rewritten with python3 os.setsid() after fork(), the A/B is unambiguous: origin/main RESOLVED exit_code=0 at 8168ms, my round-2 build REJECTED at 5074ms. FIXED BY GATING ON THE ENTRY PATH rather than by rewording, because wording alone would have left the regression: on the exit path the command has already finished, its streams will close, and waiting is both correct and what main does -- there is no runaway left to bound. After the gate: RESOLVED exit_code=0 at 8143ms (main 8168ms), and the timeout-path bound still holds (REJECTED at 5408ms). Pinned by a regression test that asserts the observation RESOLVES and that it WAITED past the holder rather than settling at the grace budget. THE FIRST INJECTION OF THAT TEST WAS INVALID AND IS DISCLOSED: reverting the gate left `reason` unused, tsc failed TS6133, build exit=2, and the resulting test failure ran against STALE BUILD OUTPUT -- a compile error reading as a catch, which proves nothing. Re-injected in a form that compiles (build exit=0, 0 TS errors) and the test then reds correctly. ONE CORRECTION TO MY OWN EARLIER REPORT OF THAT INJECTION: I quoted the failure text as 'observed command exceeded 600000ms (entry: exit)'. That was the real output OF MY INJECTION, but the '(entry: <reason>)' fragment was instrumentation I added to keep `reason` referenced so the injection would compile -- it is NOT in the shipped message, which reads 'observed command exceeded <timeoutMs>ms, its process group could not be signalled, and it did not close its streams within a further <killGraceMs>ms'. Quoting it without saying so invited the reader to believe the shipped code says it; flagged by review and corrected here and in the PR body. THREE CLAIMS CORRECTED THIS ROUND. (1) My EPERM frequency comment recorded 300/300 ESRCH without its conditions. It has now been WRONG THREE TIMES and round 4 settled it: the condition is an UNREAPED (ZOMBIE) CHILD in the group -- a zombie's credentials are cleared so it cannot be signalled. Verified independently here, sequentially, before writing it: bash -c 'printf x' (leader only) x200 -> EPERM 0; bash -c 'sleep 0.05 & printf x' (LIVE second member) x200 -> EPERM 0; bash -c 'true & printf x' (unreaped child) x200 -> ESRCH 143, EPERM 48, SIGNALLED 9 = 24%. My round-3 'more than one member' framing was therefore WRONG and dangerous in the false all-clear direction -- a live second member gives 0 -- and its causality was inverted: it is the CHILD going unreaped, not the leader being reaped. My round-3 comment also asserted that a loop over `bash -lc true` 'will never show it'; review measured that exact command at 3/3200 (~0.09%) and my own run got 0/1600, which at that rate is unremarkable and does not contradict it -- neither observation supports the word 'never', and that is precisely the sentence a future reader would cite to dismiss a valid probe. Both earlier rate figures (review's 1.75%, my 2/800) are RETRACTED AS RATES; the mechanism and the reproducer survive. (2) The test header's disclosure that a real EPERM 'needs a process group owned by another uid' was FALSE and is retired: real EPERM occurs at the same uid with no privilege change, and the actual reason for stubbing is that 2-in-800 is too rare to assert on deterministically. (3) Two claims I corrected in the source comment in round 2 had never reached the test file; they are corrected there now. TWO FOLLOW-UPS FILED, 